  "stories": [
    {
      "id": "coffee",
      "kicker": "Coffee country",
      "title": "Seven beans from Mocha, a forest that learned to drink",
      "image": "assets/coffee-shade.jpg",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Local tradition holds that the Sufi saint Baba Budan carried seven raw coffee seeds from Mocha, in present-day Yemen, and planted them in these hills. Whether every detail of the legend can be proved, the agricultural fact is stubborn: this district became the heart of Indian arabica, grown in the shade of silver oak and native canopy rather than in open sun.",
        "What you see from a ridge is not wilderness alone. It is a polyculture — coffee, pepper, sometimes cardamom — stitched into shola and grassland. The work is seasonal, the labour skilled, and the landscape older than the cafés that made the name famous.",
        "This companion treats coffee as culture and ecology, not as a room to book. If an estate opens a walk, that is their hospitality. Walk where you are invited."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "culture",
      "kicker": "Culture",
      "title": "Hoysala stone, Malnad Kannada, a matha on the Tunga",
      "image": "assets/amruthapura.jpg",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Chikkamagaluru’s east leans toward the Hoysala world: Amruthapura’s Amrutesvara temple of 1196 CE, Belavadi’s Veeranarayana trikuta, and the nearby orbit of Halebidu and Belur. The west and south fold into Malnad pilgrimage — Sringeri Sharada Peetham, Horanadu, Kalasa — where Sanskrit learning and Kannada village life share the same river terrace.",
        "Kannada is the language of the district. English is understood in town; it thins in the ghats. A greeting in Kannada is not decoration. It is how this place speaks to itself."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "food",
      "kicker": "Food",
      "title": "Akki, koli, and a cup that still tastes of the hill",
      "image": "assets/coffee-estate.jpg",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Malnad cooking is rice-first: akki rotti, kadubu, forest greens, pork or koli (chicken) in village kitchens, fish where the Bhadra and Tunga allow. Temple towns such as Horanadu are known for annadana — a meal as offering — with timings set by the temple, not by a tourism brochure.",
        "Filter coffee here is not a brand story. It is boiled milk, a dark decoction, and beans that grew under trees you can still see from the bus window. Ask where the beans were picked. That is the only authenticity test that matters."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "responsible",
      "kicker": "Responsible travel",
      "title": "A biodiversity hotspot is not a backdrop",
      "image": "assets/kudremukh-np.jpg",
      "paragraphs": [
        "These hills sit in the Western Ghats, a UNESCO World Heritage biodiversity hotspot. Shola grassland is easy to scar and slow to heal. Stay on existing paths. Do not cut across meadows for a photograph. Pack every wrapper back to town.",
        "Elephants, gaur and, in Bhadra, tigers use this district. Drive as if a large animal owns the next bend — because it might. Do not play loudspeakers on ridges. Do not light fires. Kudremukh and Bhadra are notified forests: permission is not a suggestion.",
        "This is not an official government website. It does not inspect hotels, sell rooms, or take payments. For closures, fees and permits, use the district, forest and Karnataka Tourism pages linked throughout."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "essentials": {
    "access": {
      "title": "How to arrive",
      "items": [
        {
          "title": "By road",
          "text": "Chikkamagaluru town sits in the coffee foothills of the Chandra Drona range. Bengaluru is a long ghat-and-maidan drive (often quoted around five and a half hours via Hassan or Kadur). Mysuru and Mangaluru also have regular buses. Treat any hour-count as weather-dependent."
        },
        {
          "title": "By rail",
          "text": "Chikkamagaluru has a railway station with passenger services connecting via Kadur. Many travellers still alight at Kadur or Birur and continue by bus or taxi. Check current Indian Railways timetables — this page does not list train numbers as if they were fixed."
        },
        {
          "title": "By air",
          "text": "Mangaluru International Airport is the usual coastal gateway; Bengaluru for the plateau. There is no district airport implied here."
        }
      ]
    },
    "transport": {
      "title": "Moving around",
      "items": [
        {
          "title": "Buses",
          "text": "KSRTC and private buses cover taluk towns. Frequency drops on Sundays and in heavy rain. For remote falls, a town bus is rarely the last mile."
        },
        {
          "title": "Self-drive and taxis",
          "text": "Ghat roads are narrow, blind and used by buses, jeeps and wildlife. Fuel up in town. Jeep tracks to Jhari or Hebbe are not ordinary car roads."
        },
        {
          "title": "Maps",
          "text": "OpenStreetMap on this page is a reference overlay, not a trail warranty. Offline maps help when the ridge loses signal."
        }
      ]
    },
    "permits": {
      "title": "Forests, fees, permits",
      "items": [
        {
          "title": "National parks and tiger reserve",
          "text": "Kudremukh National Park and Bhadra Tiger Reserve generally require permission from the Karnataka Forest Department. Seasonal closures, trek caps and safari rules are theirs to set. This companion does not quote prices or guarantee that a walk is open."
        },
        {
          "title": "Estates and private land",
          "text": "Coffee country is privately worked. A viewpoint on social media may sit inside an estate. Ask; do not climb fences."
        },
        {
          "title": "Where to check",
          "text": "Start with the district tourism pages and the forest department. If a blog and an official PDF disagree, believe the PDF."
        }
      ]
    },
    "emergency": {
      "title": "Help and emergency",
      "items": [
        {
          "title": "National emergency number",
          "text": "Dial 112 in India for police, fire and ambulance coordination."
        },
        {
          "title": "District helplines",
          "text": "The Chikkamagaluru district administration publishes helpline and directory pages. Use them for current police, hospital and disaster-management contacts — numbers change."
        },
        {
          "title": "On the road",
          "text": "Wikivoyage and local advice agree on a few unglamorous points: fill the tank before remote loops, watch for elephants, and do not enter flooded fords in the monsoon."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "guide": {
    "title": "A local visitor’s skim",
    "intro": "A short, unofficial briefing you can read over coffee before you leave town. Nothing here replaces an official notice.",
    "cards": [
      {
        "title": "Give the peaks a morning",
        "text": "Mullayanagiri and Kemmanagundi reward an early start. Cloud builds through the day. If you only walk one ridge, walk it before the buses stack on the hairpins."
      },
      {
        "title": "Do not stack every waterfall",
        "text": "Hebbe, Jhari and Sirimane are different corners of the district. A single day that ‘does them all’ is how people drive tired into a ghats dusk."
      },
      {
        "title": "Temple towns are not viewpoints",
        "text": "Sringeri and Horanadu are living mathas and temples. Plan them as pilgrimages with dress codes, not as selfie stops bolted onto a trek."
      },
      {
        "title": "Monsoon is a different country",
        "text": "If you come for falling water, come ready to abandon the plan. A closed forest gate is the correct ending to some days."
      },
      {
        "title": "Learn two Kannada words",
        "text": "Namaskara. Dhanyavadagalu. They open more doors than an English itinerary shouted over a jeep engine."
      },
      {
        "title": "Leave the booking to the department",
        "text": "Homestays, resorts and rooms are intentionally absent here. The district tourism pages point toward accommodation that the administration chooses to list."
      }
    ]
  },
